---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HorizontalCard from "../components/HorizontalCard.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import createSlug from "../lib/createSlug"
const posts = (await getCollection("blog")).sort((a, b) => b.data.pubDate.valueOf() - a.data.pubDate.valueOf());
const last_posts = posts.slice(0, 3);
---

<BaseLayout sideBarActiveItemID="home">
  <div class="pb-12 mt-5">
    <div class="text-xl py-1">Hey there 👋</div>
    <div class="text-3xl font-bold">I'm Zhen Xu</div>

    <div class="py-2 mt-8 text-lg space-y-4">
      <p>
        I’m a PhD candidate in Cognitive Science at Columbia University.
      </p>

      <p>
        My research focuses on AI for Education and Human Skill Development. Drawing on cognitive science, learning sciences, and pedagogical theory, I seek to understand how AI is reshaping human learning and to advance AI as a meaningful cognitive partner that fosters human growth.
      </p>

      <p>Specifically, my work follows two interconnected strands:</p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>
          <span class="font-semibold">Understanding human cognition and behavior in the age of AI.</span>
          I study emerging patterns of human–AI interaction in real-world educational and knowledge-work settings, examining how AI reshapes <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22282" target="_blank" class="underline">learning behaviors</a>, <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3774398.3811556" target="_blank" class="underline">cognitive engagement</a>, social interaction, and teaching practices, and what new opportunities and challenges these changes create.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="font-semibold">Evaluating and advancing AI for learning and skill development.</span>
          My recent work focuses on <a href="https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-emnlp.518.pdf" target="_blank" class="underline">evaluating</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09667" target="_blank" class="underline">improving</a> AI’s pedagogical capabilities and simulating human cognition and behavior, with the goal of building more effective and adaptive AI support across diverse learning contexts.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Beyond these two strands, I am also interested in applying cognitive science to understand and improve AI reasoning and human–AI collaboration.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am fortunate to be advised by Dr. <a href="https://renzheyu.com/" target="_blank" class="underline">Renzhe Yu</a> and Dr. <a href="https://www.tc.columbia.edu/faculty/jec34/" target="_blank" class="underline">James Corter</a>. Previously, I received my master’s degree in STEM Education from the <a href="https://physics.bnu.edu.cn/" target="_blank" class="underline">School of Physics and Astronomy</a>, <a href="https://english.bnu.edu.cn/" target="_blank" class="underline">Beijing Normal University</a>, where I worked with Dr. <a href="https://physics.bnu.edu.cn/szdw/wlx/ltxjs/f3a08956e51f42d3a05f5e0737f9c347.htm" target="_blank" class="underline">Ping Zhang</a> on developing assessment instruments for complex concept learning and scientific reasoning. I also worked as a PhD research intern at the Educational Testing Service (ETS) Research Institute, where I collaborated with <a href="https://www.ets.org/research/author-bio/Mikeska--Jamie-N.html" target="_blank" class="underline">Jamie Mikeska</a> and <a href="https://www.ets.org/research/author-bio/Beigman-Klebanov-Beata.html" target="_blank" class="underline">Beata Beigman Klebanov</a> on AI-based learner simulation for teacher training.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

<div class="mt-10">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2">📢 News</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-base space-y-1 leading-relaxed">
    <li>2026.10 – Our paper on AI-based learner simulation was accepted to the <a href="https://iab-agents.github.io/" target="_blank" class="underline">Interpreting Agent Behavior (IAB) Workshop</a> at <a href="https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2026" target="_blank" class="underline">NeurIPS 2026</a> in Sydney!</li>
    <li>2026.06 – I will start my summer research intern at <a href="https://www.ets.org/research.html" target="_blank" class="underline">ETS</a>!</li>  
    <li>2026.05 – Our paper received the Best Full Paper Award 🏆 at the <a href="https://edsconference.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" class="underline">Stanford Education Data Science Conference</a>!</li> 
    <li>2026.03 – Our <a href="https://www.solaresearch.org/events/lak/lak26/" target="_blank" class="underline">LAK 2026</a> paper on explainable AI–based data annotation received a Best Full Paper nomination 🏅!</li>
    <li>2026.02 – I will co-organize the LAK 2026 workshop <a href="https://sites.google.com/view/lak26-caworkshop/home" target="_blank" class="underline">“Curriculum Analytics for 21st-Century Competencies in Postsecondary Education”</a> in Bergen, Norway.</li>
    <li>2025.07 – I will attend <a href="https://aied2025.itd.cnr.it/" target="_blank" class="underline">AIED</a>, <a href="https://educationaldatamining.org/edm2025/" target="_blank" class="underline">EDM</a>, and <a href="https://learningatscale.acm.org/las2025/" target="_blank" class="underline">L@S</a> in July 2025 in Palermo, Italy. Happy to connect if you'll be there too!</li>
    <li>2025.04 – I will give an online talk as a Guest Speaker and Panelist at the Educational Technology Sub-Forum of the 16th China Education Symposium at Harvard University.</li>
    <li>2025.03 – I will give an online talk as a Guest Speaker at the AI Application Sub-Forum of Columbia University Teachers College China-US Education Forum.</li>
    <li>2024.11 – Our paper “Intersectional inequalities in asynchronous academic communication” won Best Student Paper Awards at <a href="https://computationinpsych.com/" target="_blank" class="underline">SCiP 2024</a> 🏆.</li>
    <li>2024.09 – I will start my PhD in Cognitive Science at <a href="https://www.tc.columbia.edu/" target="_blank" class="underline">Teachers College, Columbia University</a>!</li>
  </ul>
</div>

